{
"dur": 55.548,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 11.28,
"text": "Back in twenty twenty-two, the whole hobby was hunting for one card. A one-of-one LeBron James card carrying logo patches from three of his teams. Collectors called it the golden ticket.",
"end": 11.555
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 11.555,
"vo_start": 11.83,
"vo_end": 18.898,
"text": "Boxes were ripped open all over the world. Even celebrities joined in, opening boxes worth six figures live online, just to find it.",
"end": 19.173
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 19.173,
"vo_start": 19.448,
"vo_end": 26.686,
"text": "And Whatnot made a promise. If that card was pulled in a break on its platform, the seller would win a brand new Lamborghini.",
"end": 26.961
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 26.961,
"vo_start": 27.236,
"vo_end": 36.196,
"text": "Then it actually happened. Three complete strangers, each holding a random team in a Whatnot break, watched the seller pull it. The card carried all three of their teams.",
"end": 36.471
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 36.471,
"vo_start": 36.746,
"vo_end": 45.568,
"text": "It later sold at auction for around two point four million dollars, split three ways. Life-changing money, from spots that cost a tiny fraction of that.",
"end": 45.843
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 45.843,
"vo_start": 46.118,
"vo_end": 54.598,
"text": "A win like that is the best advert a platform could dream of. But in a documentary about the hunt, the presenter said it plainly: box breaks are the hobby's form of gambling.",
"end": 55.548
}
],
